## Problem 4 — building an argv you can defend

Edit `ex4/solution.ts`. Implement:

```ts
export interface SpawnSpec { file: string; args: string[]; shell: boolean; }

export function planSpawn(input: {
  file: string;
  args: readonly string[];
  platform?: NodeJS.Platform;
}): SpawnSpec
```

The policy:

1. arguments are passed through untouched — a value containing `;`, `|`,
   `$(...)` or a backtick is *data*, and the returned `shell` stays `false`;
2. `file` must be a plain program name or path with no shell metacharacters
   at all: `; | & $ \` ( ) < > \n` in `file` throws an `Error` whose message
   mentions `metacharacter`;
3. on `win32` only, a `file` ending in `.cmd` or `.bat` requires
   `shell: true` — set it, because Node cannot run batch files any other way;
4. an empty `file` throws a `RangeError`.

```ts
planSpawn({ file: "grep", args: ["; rm -rf /", "notes.txt"] });
// { file: "grep", args: ["; rm -rf /", "notes.txt"], shell: false }

planSpawn({ file: "npm.cmd", args: ["run", "build"], platform: "win32" });
// { file: "npm.cmd", args: ["run", "build"], shell: true }

planSpawn({ file: "sh -c 'x'", args: [] });   // throws: "...metacharacter..."
```

```
